{:edb/db-before-t 1967 :edb/db-after-t 1968 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535280 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:09:58.605Z" 13194139535280 true] [17592186149788 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149790 13194139535280 true] [17592186149791 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149792 13194139535280 true] [17592186149794 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149795 13194139535280 true] [17592186149797 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149798 13194139535280 true] [17592186149800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149801 13194139535280 true] [17592186149803 1104 #edb/ref 17592186149804 13194139535280 true] [17592186151885 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186151885 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n1. $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n2. $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n3. $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" 13194139535280 false] [17592186151885 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x$. If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n$(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.\n- The equation\n$y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x$, which is different from zero.\n- Writing the equation\n$y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f$, we have\n$y'' + x^3 y = 8$.\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8$, which is different from zero." 13194139535280 false] [17592186151885 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero." 13194139535280 true] [17592186151886 1100 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186151886 1100 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n1. $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n2. $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n3. $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" 13194139535280 false] [17592186151886 1145 "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n$y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'$.\n\n- The equation\n$y''+y'=x \\cos x$\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0$, and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}$.\n- The equation\n$yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535280 false] [17592186151886 1145 "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535280 true] [17592186151887 1100 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186151887 1100 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n2. $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(4)= 1$\n3. $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1)= 5$" 13194139535280 false] [17592186151887 1145 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y$, then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4$. So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535280 false] [17592186151887 1145 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535280 true] [17592186151888 1100 "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139535280 false] [17592186151888 1100 "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139535280 true] [17592186151888 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0\n$$" 13194139535280 false] [17592186151888 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186152198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341351 13194139535280 true] [17592186152198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341358 13194139535280 true] [17592186152198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341416 13194139535280 true] [17592186152198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341444 13194139535280 true] [17592186152198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341451 13194139535280 true] [17592186152199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341386 13194139535280 true] [17592186152199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341396 13194139535280 true] [17592186152199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341406 13194139535280 true] [17592186152200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341365 13194139535280 true] [17592186152200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341372 13194139535280 true] [17592186152200 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341458 13194139535280 true] [17592186152201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341379 13194139535280 true] [17592186152201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341423 13194139535280 true] [17592186152201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341430 13194139535280 true] [17592186152201 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341437 13194139535280 true] [17592186152488 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186152488 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139535280 true] [17592186152488 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\nThe equation $\\sin (x)y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + y = 2$ is {{field-2}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} + xy - 2x = 0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139535280 false] [17592186152488 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=2x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2x,$ which is different from zero." 13194139535280 true] [17592186152489 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186152489 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous. The equation $x^{2}y^{″} + 3xy^{′} - y = 0$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} - y^{′} + y = e^{x}$ is {{field-2}}.\n\nThe equation $y^{″} + x^{2}y + x = 0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139535280 false] [17592186152489 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139535280 true] [17592186152489 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=-x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-x,$ which is different from zero." 13194139535280 true] [17592186152550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186152550 1100 "For the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = x^{3}$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139535280 false] [17592186152550 1100 "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y={x}^{3},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139535280 true] [17592186152550 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186152553 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186152553 1100 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186152553 1100 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $xy^{″} - 2y^{′} + y = x^{2} + \\sin x, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 1$\n2. $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 10$\n3. $x^{2}y^{″} - 4xy^{′} + 16y = 0, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(2) = 0$" 13194139535280 false] [17592186152553 1145 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**" 13194139535280 true] [17592186152554 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186152554 1100 "For the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = e^{x}$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139535280 false] [17592186152554 1100 "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" 13194139535280 true] [17592186152554 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+3y=0.\n$$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341351 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341352 13194139535280 true] [17592186341351 1089 "q-130671" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341351 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186341351 1100 "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=2$\n- ${y}^{3}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+xy{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341351 1113 #uuid "3e02d60f-4fa6-583c-af1a-2b15f3ba4c02" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341351 1145 "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=2.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-xy=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{3}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+xy{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $xy{y}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341352 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341353 13194139535280 true] [17592186341352 