rue] [17592186341393 1041 "field-3" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341395 13194139535280 true] [17592186341393 1110 #uuid "fee27974-b815-5e20-b40c-bd897c398114" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341394 1034 "homogeneous" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535280 true] [17592186341394 1111 #uuid "8e407a07-7097-5845-9355-c9ad8823438e" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341395 1034 "inhomogeneous" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535280 true] [17592186341395 1111 #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341397 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341400 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341403 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1089 "q-338006" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1100 "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+xy=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=4x$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=0$ is {{field-3}}." 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1113 #uuid "9bd10097-6fe9-5533-b23b-68beefc52771" 13194139535280 true] [17592186341396 1145 "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+xy=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=4x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=4x,$ which is different from zero.\